  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 129 AM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nMedium Range models remain in good agreement for much of mid to\nlate week as weak to moderate southwest flow aloft dominates the\nweather pattern across the northern Front Range. With a lack of\nPacific (shortwave) energy embedded in the mean southwest flow\naloft through Friday, expect generally dry conditions\nand mild temperatures Wednesday through Friday as the polar jet\nstream will remain well to the north, and the subtropical jet\nshould stay well south of the region near the Mexico/USA border and \ninto Texas. Since southeast Wyoming and western Nebraska will be in \nbetween storm tracks, relatively mild temperatures are expected \nthrough the end of the week with afternoon highs in the upper 50s to \nupper 60s. Temperatures should peak Friday afternoon as 700mb \ntemperatures climb over 5c ahead of the next Pacific storm system \nmoving onshore, with a few locations possibly reaching 70 degrees \neast of the Interstate 25 corridor.\n\nDeterministic models are in better agreement for next weekend,\nbut ensembles still depict relatively low confidence with\nprecipitation chances...mainly due to the evolution and speed \nof the Pacific energy moving onshore. Thankfully, with the \nPacific energy trending a little slower, Halloween evening looks\ndecent with maybe a few rain showers in the evening. \nTemperatures should be relatively mild for this time of the year\nin the upper 30s to upper 40s between sunset and 10:00 PM. \nWinds appear light and variable and should result in a pleasant \nevening. Models do show the first of a series of upper level\ndisturbances lifting northward across the area on Halloween, but\nthe system is pretty progressive with the best dynamics located\nnorth and west of the I-25 corridor. A more potent system is\nforecast to arrive on Sunday and continue early next week. Model\nsolutions and ensemble members diverge significantly with this\nstorm system, so will not go into too much detail at this time.\nHowever, temperatures will trend cooler and likely below average\nfor this time of the year by next Sunday and Monday.",
